# Flutter UI Standards (Screens and Widgets)

- Prefer `ConsumerWidget`; use `ConsumerStatefulWidget` only for local ephemeral UI state (controllers, focus, animation, temporary toggles).
- Keep `build` pure: no direct navigation, dialogs, or provider mutations inside `build`.
- Standardize async states as `loading`, `error`, and `content` with visible user feedback for failures.
- Use one navigation style per flow and avoid scattering route decisions across multiple widget layers.
- Keep screens as orchestrators: delegate business rules and data transformations to providers/services.
- Use shared design tokens from `NeumorphicStyle`/theme; avoid one-off visual constants in feature widgets.
- Favor composable private UI sections or reusable widgets when a screen becomes large and repetitive.

## Examples

```dart
// BAD: side effect in build
@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
  if (isLoggedIn) {
    Navigator.pushReplacement(...);
  }
  return const SizedBox.shrink();
}
```

```dart
// GOOD: side effect from lifecycle/listener with guard
@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) {
    if (!mounted) return;
    if (isLoggedIn) Navigator.pushReplacement(...);
  });
}
```

```dart
// GOOD: mounted check after await before UI side effects
Future<void> submit() async {
  setState(() => _isLoading = true);
  try {
    await ref.read(authProvider).signIn();
    if (!mounted) return;
    Navigator.pushReplacement(...);
  } finally {
    if (mounted) setState(() => _isLoading = false);
  }
}
```
